 "article": {
  "title": "The Woman Who Mapped the Ocean Floor",
  "topic": "人物小传（科学史）",
  "level": "CET-4",
  "words": 320,
  "paras": [
   "Marie Tharp did not set out to rewrite the history of the Earth. Born in 1920 in Michigan, she studied music first and geology later, at a time when very few women entered science. In 1948 she moved to New York and took a job at Columbia University, where she was hired to draw maps. She was not, however, allowed to go to sea on the research ships, because women were then thought to bring bad luck on board.",
   "So Tharp stayed on land and worked with numbers. Ships crossing the Atlantic sent back long strips of sonar readings, careful measurements of the depth of the sea floor. For years she turned those columns of figures into drawings. Slowly, a hidden landscape appeared: great mountains, wide plains, and a deep valley running down the middle of the ocean. That valley, the rift of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, had never been seen before.",
   "The finding mattered a great deal. If the sea floor was splitting apart along that rift, then the continents really could be drifting, an idea that many scientists still laughed at. Tharp's colleague Bruce Heezen at first dismissed her discovery as \"girl talk\". She simply kept checking her work. When the evidence could no longer be ignored, he became one of her strongest supporters, and together they published a map that changed how we see our planet.",
   "Tharp spent about thirty years painting the invisible. In 1977 her map, redrawn by the artist Heinrich Berann, appeared in textbooks around the world. Today many call her a founder of modern oceanography. Her story is a reminder that a great discovery may begin not with a ship, but with patience, a sharp pencil, and a refusal to stop asking questions."
  ],
  "zh": [
   "玛丽·萨普起初并不是要改写地球的历史。她 1920 年生于密歇根，先学音乐、后学地质，那个年代进入科学界的女性寥寥无几。1948 年她搬到纽约，在哥伦比亚大学谋得一份绘制地图的工作。然而她不被允许随科考船出海——因为当时人们认为女人上船会带来厄运。",
   "于是萨普留在陆地上与数字打交道。横渡大西洋的船只传回一长条一长条的声呐读数，那是关于海底深度的精确测量。年复一年，她把这些成列的数字变成图形。一幅隐藏的地貌缓缓显现：高耸的山脉、广阔的平原，以及一条纵贯大洋中部的深谷。那条深谷——大西洋中脊的裂谷——此前从未被人看到。",
   "这个发现意义重大。如果海底是沿着那条裂谷张开的，那么大陆果真可能在漂移——而这个想法当时仍被许多科学家嘲笑。萨普的同事布鲁斯·希森起初把她的发现斥为“女孩的闲话”。她只是继续一遍遍核对自己的工作。当证据再也无法被忽视时，他成了她最坚定的支持者之一，两人一起发表了一张改变我们看待这个星球方式的图。",
   "萨普用了大约三十年描画那看不见的世界。1977 年，她的地图经艺术家海因里希·贝兰重绘后，出现在世界各地的教科书里。今天，许多人称她为现代海洋学的奠基人之一。她的故事提醒我们：一项伟大的发现，也许并非始于一艘船，而是始于耐心、一支削尖的铅笔，以及一颗不肯停止追问的心。"
  ],
  "hard": [
   {
    "en": "Born in 1920 in Michigan, she studied music first and geology later, at a time when very few women entered science.",
    "cn": "她 1920 年生于密歇根，先学音乐、后学地质，而那时进入科学界的女性寥寥无几。",
    "why": "句首的过去分词短语 Born in 1920… 作状语，交代人物背景；at a time when… 是“介词＋关系副词 when”引导的定语从句，修饰 time。写作时可套用“Born in…, sb. did…, at a time when…”来简洁交代人物出身与时代。"
   },
   {
    "en": "If the sea floor was splitting apart along that rift, then the continents really could be drifting, an idea that many scientists still laughed at.",
    "cn": "如果海底是沿着那条裂谷张开的，那么大陆果真可能在漂移——而这个想法仍被许多科学家嘲笑。",
    "why": "If 引导的条件句与主句构成推演；句末 an idea that… 是同位语，that 引导定语从句补充说明 idea。适合用来“先给条件、再引出被质疑的观点”。"
   },
   {
    "en": "Her story is a reminder that a great discovery may begin not with a ship, but with patience, a sharp pencil, and a refusal to stop asking questions.",
    "cn": "她的故事提醒我们：一项伟大的发现，也许并非始于一艘船，而是始于耐心、一支削尖的铅笔，以及一颗不肯停止追问的心。",
    "why": "a reminder that… 引导同位语从句；not…but… 形成对比强调。是结尾点题的万能句式，可套用“… is a reminder that success may begin not with A, but with B, C and D.”。"
   }
  ],
